    "narrative": "Ghost Ride the WHIP enters today\u2019s slate prioritizing stolen base upside and favorable platoon matchups. Our strongest play is **James Wood** (76.27), who benefits from a prime leadoff spot and a high-yield matchup against the lefty Rodriguez. **Wood** is our focal point for speed today, as the 85% SB success rate against the opposing battery significantly juices his score. Similarly, **Austin Riley** (73.19) is a must-start; his score is buoyed by a substantial 16% park factor boost and the fact that he is facing a pitcher in Rogers whose delivery profile facilitates a 91% SB opportunity for our baserunners. **Brice Turang** (72.70) also projects as an elite contributor, taking advantage of a high 5.12 SP_xERA from Sugano, allowing him to capitalize on his own speed profile against a pitcher who struggles to neutralize runners.\n\nThe most difficult decision on the bench was **Mickey Moniak**, who fell just short of the 42.0 threshold with a 41.19 rating. While his profile typically fits our mandate, the combination of a difficult matchup against the lefty Drohan and a significant 15% penalty in our dynamic platoon modeling kept him off the field today. We also had to hold the line on **Vinnie Pasquantino** (39.33) and **Travis Bazzana** (39.29), as both fell below our 40-point efficiency floor due to prohibitive matchups against tough arms in Skubal and McClanahan. Owners should remain vigilant regarding **Edwin Arroyo** (40.42); he narrowly missed the starting cut, and his status is further complicated by a 42% rain risk in his contest. With **Gabriel Moreno** and **Corbin Carroll** facing the 3.45 xERA of Palmquist, we are closely monitoring the impact of that specific arm on our projected efficiency, though their speed remains an essential component of our daily strategy.",
